<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ottokar II of Bohemia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1233–<a href="/years/1278">1278</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Přemysl Otakar II · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/premyslid-dynasty">Přemyslid dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ottokar II was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> from 1253, known as the Iron and Golden King for his military strength and wealth. He took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1251 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a> after the Babenberg line died out and built a realm stretching to the Adriatic. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-i-of-germany">Rudolf of Habsburg</a> stripped him of these lands and defeated and killed him at the Marchfeld in 1278.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ottokar-ii-of-bohemia.jpg" alt="Ottokar II of Bohemia" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Otakarus · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PecetpoII.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Teutonic Knights’ fortress of Königsberg, founded in 1255, is traditionally said to have been named in honour of King <b>Ottokar II of Bohemia</b>, who had joined their crusade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Afterwards Béla IV encouraged stone castles, which helped repel a second Mongol invasion in 1285–1286, and in 1278 Ladislaus IV's cavalry helped Rudolf of Habsburg defeat <b>Ottokar II of Bohemia</b> on the Marchfeld.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1000-1301">Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Mongol raiders crossed it in 1241, and in 1278 <b>Ottokar II of Bohemia</b>, who had used Moravia as a base for his Austrian ambitions, was killed fighting Rudolf of Habsburg at the Battle on the Marchfeld, just beyond the Moravian border.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-moravia">Margraviate of Moravia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Its conquest was secured by a dense network of castles, at first of timber and earth and later of brick, from Thorn and Marienwerder to Königsberg, founded in 1255 during a crusade led by King <b>Ottokar II of Bohemia</b> and named in his honour.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-the-teutonic-order">State of the Teutonic Order · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>At Dürnkrut and Jedenspeigen, the German king Rudolf I of Habsburg, allied with King Ladislaus IV of Hungary, defeated and killed <b>Ottokar II of Bohemia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-26">26 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Count Rudolf IV was elected King of the Romans in 1273 as Rudolf I and in 1278 defeated <b>Ottokar II of Bohemia</b>, gaining Austria and Styria.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-habsburg">County of Habsburg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Městec Králové, in what is now <a href="/countries/czech-republic">the Czech Republic</a>. Died in Dürnkrut, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.21,15.3&amp;year=1262" aria-label="Městec Králové on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4900 418 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ottokar II of Bohemia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5350 688h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5350 688h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.21,15.3&amp;year=1262">What was here in 1262? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
